  'dahshur-pyramids-egypt',
  'Dahshur Pyramids Egypt: Saqqara Without the Crowds',
  '<p>At Giza you queue to enter a pyramid. At Dahshur you can often walk into one alone, and then walk into a second one alone half an hour later.</p>

<p>That is the whole argument for spending a morning here. The dahshur pyramids egypt itineraries leave out are about forty kilometres south of Cairo, they are older than Giza, and on a weekday in winter you may share them with a dozen people.</p>

<h2>Why These Two Sites Are Quiet</h2>

<p>Three reasons, and none of them is that the sites are less interesting.</p>

<p>Dahshur sat inside a military zone until 1996 and only reached the standard Cairo circuit slowly. Habit is durable in tourism, and most half day pyramid tours still go Giza and back.</p>

<p>Saqqara is better known but usually gets an hour tacked onto a Giza morning, which is enough for the stepped pyramid from a distance and nothing else.</p>

<p>And both sites are spread out. Giza puts three pyramids and a sphinx inside a walkable area; Dahshur has two pyramids nearly two kilometres apart across open sand. That geography keeps coaches away, and it is exactly why it feels the way it does.</p>

<h2>The Bent Pyramid, and Why It Looks Wrong</h2>

<p>Sneferu''s first pyramid at Dahshur changes angle about halfway up, from roughly 54 degrees to roughly 43. From a distance it looks like a building that lost its nerve.</p>

<p>The usual explanation is structural. Cracks appeared during construction, the angle was reduced to lighten the upper mass, and the result is the only pyramid in Egypt with a visible change of mind in it. Some of the original polished limestone casing survives on the lower courses, which almost nowhere else has.</p>

<p>You can go inside. The entrance is on the north face, the descent is steep and narrow, and it opens into two chambers with corbelled ceilings rising above you in tightening rings of stone. It is one of the more physically demanding interiors in Egypt and one of the most rewarding.</p>

<p>Bring a torch even though there is lighting. The lighting is intermittent.</p>

<h2>The Red Pyramid, Which You Should Definitely Enter</h2>

<p>Sneferu''s third attempt, a kilometre or so north, is the first successful true pyramid and the third largest in Egypt after the two big ones at Giza. Its name comes from the reddish limestone showing through where the casing was stripped.</p>

<p>The interior is the reason to come. A long descending passage, then three chambers, the last of them with a corbelled ceiling twelve metres high. It is vast, empty and silent, and unlike Giza there is usually nobody behind you hurrying you along.</p>

<p>The descent is about 60 metres at a steady crouch. If you are claustrophobic this is not the one to test yourself on, and if you have one pyramid interior in you, spend it here rather than at Giza.</p>

<h2>What Saqqara Adds</h2>

<p>Saqqara is a different kind of site: not a few monuments but a necropolis used for more than three thousand years, spread across several square kilometres.</p>

<p>The stepped pyramid dominates it and the surrounding complex has been heavily restored, which divides opinion. The colonnade entrance and the courtyard are worth the walk regardless.</p>

<p>What most visitors miss at any saqqara pyramid egypt site is the private tombs. The mastabas of Ti, Mereruka and Kagemni carry relief carving of daily life, cattle, fishing, brewing, carpentry, that is crisper and more legible than almost anything in the royal tombs at Luxor. They are small, they are usually empty, and they are the best thing here.</p>

<p>Then there is the Serapeum: a vast underground gallery cut for the burial of sacred bulls, with granite sarcophagi weighing around seventy tonnes each sitting in side chambers. It is cool, strange and completely unlike anything else in Egypt.</p>

<h2>How the Two Sites Compare</h2>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Saqqara</th><th>Dahshur</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Distance from Cairo</td><td>About 30 km</td><td>About 40 km</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time needed</td><td>2 to 3 hours</td><td>1.5 to 2 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crowds</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Very light</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyramid interiors</td><td>Limited and variable</td><td>Two, both excellent</td></tr>
<tr><td>Highlight</td><td>The mastaba tombs and the Serapeum</td><td>The Red Pyramid interior</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking</td><td>A lot, spread out</td><td>Short walks, long drives between</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Visiting the Dahshur Pyramids Egypt Tours Skip</h2>

<p>They are close together and on the same road, so one trip covers both. The order that works is Saqqara first, Dahshur second.</p>

<p>Saqqara has more to see and more walking, so it wants the cooler hours. Dahshur is two stops and two interiors, which is the right shape for late morning when you are starting to flag.</p>

<p>Allow five hours door to door from central Cairo, including the drive. Six if you want the Serapeum and both Dahshur interiors without rushing.</p>

<p>Memphis, the ancient capital, sits between the two and takes twenty minutes. It is a small open air museum around a fallen colossus of Ramesses II. Worth stopping if it is included, not worth a detour.</p>

<h2>The Black Pyramid and What Is Left of It</h2>

<p>There is a third pyramid at Dahshur and almost nobody looks at it. Amenemhat III built it about six hundred years after Sneferu, out of mudbrick with a limestone casing, and when the casing went the core slumped.</p>

<p>What is left is a dark, eroded mound with water pooling around its base, because the local water table has risen. It is not beautiful and it is not climbable.</p>

<p>It is worth five minutes because it shows you what the other pyramids would look like without their stone. Every intact pyramid in Egypt is a survivor of the same forces that did this, and standing in front of the failure makes the successes legible.</p>

<h2>When to Go</h2>

<p>Winter mornings, on a weekday. October to April keeps the walking comfortable, and a weekday keeps the Red Pyramid interior to yourself.</p>

<p>Friday is the local day off and Saqqara gets noticeably busier with Egyptian families in the afternoon. That is pleasant rather than a problem, but it is not the empty version.</p>

<p>Summer is hard here. Both sites are open desert, the interiors are hot and airless rather than cool, and the descent into the Red Pyramid in August is a genuinely unpleasant experience. If you are here in summer, be at Saqqara at opening and finish the dahshur pyramids egypt leg before eleven.</p>

<h2>Compared with Giza</h2>

<p>This is not an either or. Giza has the Great Pyramid, the Sphinx, and a scale nothing else matches, and skipping it to be contrarian would be a mistake.</p>

<p>What Giza does not have is quiet. The plateau is the most visited site in Egypt and it feels like it from mid morning onwards.</p>

<p>The sequence that works best is Giza at sunrise on one day and Saqqara with Dahshur on another. You get the icons in good light and near silence, then you get the older, stranger version of the same story with nobody in the way. Our <a href="/12-days-egypt-tour">twelve day Egypt itinerary</a> opens with Saqqara and the Serapeum before Giza for that reason.</p>

<h2>Practical Notes</h2>

<p>Tickets are per site and the pyramid interiors at Dahshur are normally included in site entry rather than charged separately, though this changes. Current prices are <mark data-placeholder="saqqara-dahshur-tickets">FILL IN: current Saqqara and Dahshur entry prices, plus any separate Serapeum or mastaba charges</mark>, and opening hours are <mark data-placeholder="saqqara-dahshur-hours">FILL IN: current opening hours for both sites</mark>.</p>

<p>There is very little shade and almost no food at either site. Bring water. There is a cafeteria at Saqqara and effectively nothing at Dahshur.</p>

<p>You need a car and a driver. Public transport does not serve either site usefully and the distances between the Dahshur pyramids are too far to walk in the heat.</p>

<p>Both sites are dusty in a way that matters if you wear contact lenses or carry a camera with an exposed sensor.</p>

<p>One more thing about the interiors: they are hotter than the outside air, not cooler, because there is no ventilation and a lot of bodies have been through. Take the descent slowly and do not go down either pyramid straight after the other without sitting outside for a few minutes first.</p>

<h2>Who Should Skip This</h2>

<p>Worth saying plainly. If you have two days in Cairo and have never seen the Great Pyramid, spend them on Giza and the Grand Egyptian Museum. The <a href="/blog/grand-egyptian-museum-tour">museum guide</a> covers how to fit that properly.</p>

<p>If you have three days or more, or you have been to Giza before, or you specifically want to get inside a pyramid without a queue, this is the best morning available near Cairo.</p>

<p>The <a href="/egypt-travel-guide/cairo-travel-guide">Cairo area guide</a> has the wider picture on how many days the city needs.</p>

<h2>One Detail to Look For</h2>

<p>On the lower courses of the Bent Pyramid, find a surviving casing block and look at the joint between it and the next one. The gap is about the thickness of a sheet of paper.</p>

<p>Four and a half thousand years, no mortar worth speaking of, and you cannot get a fingernail in. That is the thing people remember from Dahshur, and no photograph has ever captured it.</p>',
  'An hour south of Giza there are two pyramids you can walk inside, often with nobody else in them. The Bent Pyramid is the strangest building in Egypt.',
